<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Saurashtra State</h1><p class="st-meta">1948 CE – 1956 CE · capital Rajkot</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The union that merged more than 200 Kathiawar princely states into one Indian state in 1948, with its capital at Rajkot.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Kathiawar</a> was a patchwork of over two hundred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a>, from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bhavnagar-state">Bhavnagar State</a> and Nawanagar to tiny estates. In early 1948, under pressure from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/v-p-menon">V. P. Menon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vallabhbhai-patel">Sardar Patel</a>, the rulers agreed to merge their states into the United State of Kathiawar, inaugurated in February 1948; <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/junagadh-state">Junagadh State</a> joined in 1949.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/digvijaysinhji">Jam Sahib of Nawanagar</a>, Digvijaysinhji, served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajpramukh">Rajpramukh</a>, while an elected government led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/u-n-dhebar">U. N. Dhebar</a> ran the administration from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/rajkot-state">Rajkot</a>. Renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Saurashtra</a>, it became a Part B state of the Indian Union in 1950. Its governments carried out land reform that abolished the girasdari and barkhali landholdings of the old nobility.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under the States Reorganisation Act <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Saurashtra</a> was merged into the enlarged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bombay-state">Bombay State</a> on 1 November 1956. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> was divided in 1960, the region became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/saurashtra-state.jpg" alt="Saurashtra State" width="320" height="387"><figcaption>Image: India 1951 Bombay.svg: Superbenjamin this file: Furfur · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saurashtra_in_India_(1951).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1948 CE – 1956 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Rajkot</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Digvijaysinhji</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism; Jainism; Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gujarati</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>United State of Kathiawar, United State of Saurashtra</dd></dl></aside></div>
